{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"rahimnathwani"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"I read last week's Economist.<p>I agree that mortgage interest payments should not be tax-deductible for individuals.  This distorted house prices in the UK until MIRAS was abolished many years ago, but the same situation exists today in the USA.<p>I disagreed with most of the rest, which addressed interest paid by businesses.  I don't agree that interest payments are a special type of business expense that should be treated differently from others (like rent).  If you accept that businesses should be taxed based on the profit they make, then singling out interest as a non-tax-deductible expense means that some businesses would pay profits tax, even when their accounts show zero or negative profit.  It would also create perverse incentives:<p>- If I need capital for my business, but don't want to give up control, I will have to create an additional class of shares, and issue more shares whenever I need to 'borrow' more money<p>- If I need equipment for my business, I'll try and rent it instead of buying it, even though I intend to use the equipment until it's on its last legs.  Oh, and the deal I'll strike will take this into account, so under IFRS it will be classed as a <em>finance</em> <em>lease</em>, so my controller will need to spend time explaining that to the auditors.  Yay."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Tax-Free Debt: The great distortion"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21651213-subsidies-make-borrowing-irresistible-need-be-phased-out-great-distortion"}},"_tags":["comment","author_rahimnathwani","story_9622493"],"author":"rahimnathwani","comment_text":"I read last week&#x27;s Economist.<p>I agree that mortgage interest payments should not be tax-deductible for individuals.  This distorted house prices in the UK until MIRAS was abolished many years ago, but the same situation exists today in the USA.<p>I disagreed with most of the rest, which addressed interest paid by businesses.  I don&#x27;t agree that interest payments are a special type of business expense that should be treated differently from others (like rent).  If you accept that businesses should be taxed based on the profit they make, then singling out interest as a non-tax-deductible expense means that some businesses would pay profits tax, even when their accounts show zero or negative profit.  It would also create perverse incentives:<p>- If I need capital for my business, but don&#x27;t want to give up control, I will have to create an additional class of shares, and issue more shares whenever I need to &#x27;borrow&#x27; more money<p>- If I need equipment for my business, I&#x27;ll try and rent it instead of buying it, even though I intend to use the equipment until it&#x27;s on its last legs.  Oh, and the deal I&#x27;ll strike will take this into account, so under IFRS it will be classed as a finance lease, so my controller will need to spend time explaining that to the auditors.  Yay.","created_at":"2015-05-29T08:08:37Z","created_at_i":1432886917,"objectID":"9623335","parent_id":9622747,"points":null,"story_id":9622493,"story_title":"Tax-Free Debt: The great distortion","story_url":"http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21651213-subsidies-make-borrowing-irresistible-need-be-phased-out-great-distortion","updated_at":"2023-09-07T02:51:17Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"rjtavares"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"There are rules to decide whether a <em>lease</em> counts as an asset for the company (in which case part of the rent is interest).<p>IFRS states if any of these tests are met, the <em>lease</em> is considered a <em>finance</em> <em>lease</em>:<p>-ownership of the asset is transferred to the lessee at the end of the <em>lease</em> term;<p>-the <em>lease</em> contains a bargain purchase option to buy the equipment at less than fair market value;<p>-the <em>lease</em> term is for the major part of the economic life of \nthe asset even if title is not transferred;<p>-at the inception of the <em>lease</em> the present value of the minimum <em>lease</em> payments amounts to at least substantially all of the fair value of the leased asset.<p>-the leased assets are of a specialised nature such that only  the lessee can use them without major modifications being made.<p>US GAAP has a similar provision."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Tax-Free Debt: The great distortion"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21651213-subsidies-make-borrowing-irresistible-need-be-phased-out-great-distortion"}},"_tags":["comment","author_rjtavares","story_9622493"],"author":"rjtavares","children":[9623261],"comment_text":"There are rules to decide whether a lease counts as an asset for the company (in which case part of the rent is interest).<p>IFRS states if any of these tests are met, the lease is considered a finance lease:<p>-ownership of the asset is transferred to the lessee at the end of the lease term;<p>-the lease contains a bargain purchase option to buy the equipment at less than fair market value;<p>-the lease term is for the major part of the economic life of \nthe asset even if title is not transferred;<p>-at the inception of the lease the present value of the minimum lease payments amounts to at least substantially all of the fair value of the leased asset.<p>-the leased assets are of a specialised nature such that only  the lessee can use them without major modifications being made.<p>US GAAP has a similar provision.","created_at":"2015-05-29T07:35:40Z","created_at_i":1432884940,"objectID":"9623230","parent_id":9623011,"points":null,"story_id":9622493,"story_title":"Tax-Free Debt: The great distortion","story_url":"http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21651213-subsidies-make-borrowing-irresistible-need-be-phased-out-great-distortion","updated_at":"2023-09-07T02:51:17Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"vidarh"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Altucher writes well, but I find it hard to overlook the relatively large inaccuracies in the bits I do know about, and keep wondering what else is equally inaccurate. E.g. he seems to think &quot;Virgin Air&quot; (the actual airlines are Virgin Atlantic Airways and Virgin America) is Bransons main business, but Virgin Group consists of a huge number of companies, and Virgin Atlantic revenue only accounts for about 10% of the groups aggregate revenue (Virgin Atlantic revenue is about 20% of Virgin Group revenue, but Virgin Group only owns about half of Virgin Atlantic)<p>EDIT: The story about how Branson supposedly got the idea also sounds apocryphal. In 1984 Branson was rich already - in fact Virgin Atlantic became profitable in large part because Virgin Records was doing well enough that Branson could use Virgin Records to <em>finance</em> <em>lease</em> of a secondhand plane for the company to start out with.. What was to become Virgin Atlantic was started by Randolph Fields  and Alan Hellary before Branson got involved. It's not impossible Branson was thinking of setting up an airline before that, but he didn't start Virgin Atlantic until after he'd been approached by Fields."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Elon Musk\u2019s Space Dream Almost Killed Tesla"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2015-elon-musk-spacex/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_vidarh","story_9544455"],"author":"vidarh","children":[9546523,9546661],"comment_text":"Altucher writes well, but I find it hard to overlook the relatively large inaccuracies in the bits I do know about, and keep wondering what else is equally inaccurate. E.g. he seems to think &quot;Virgin Air&quot; (the actual airlines are Virgin Atlantic Airways and Virgin America) is Bransons main business, but Virgin Group consists of a huge number of companies, and Virgin Atlantic revenue only accounts for about 10% of the groups aggregate revenue (Virgin Atlantic revenue is about 20% of Virgin Group revenue, but Virgin Group only owns about half of Virgin Atlantic)<p>EDIT: The story about how Branson supposedly got the idea also sounds apocryphal. In 1984 Branson was rich already - in fact Virgin Atlantic became profitable in large part because Virgin Records was doing well enough that Branson could use Virgin Records to finance lease of a secondhand plane for the company to start out with.. What was to become Virgin Atlantic was started by Randolph Fields  and Alan Hellary before Branson got involved. It&#x27;s not impossible Branson was thinking of setting up an airline before that, but he didn&#x27;t start Virgin Atlantic until after he&#x27;d been approached by Fields.","created_at":"2015-05-14T17:33:02Z","created_at_i":1431624782,"objectID":"9546356","parent_id":9546200,"points":null,"story_id":9544455,"story_title":"Elon Musk\u2019s Space Dream Almost Killed Tesla","story_url":"http://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2015-elon-musk-spacex/","updated_at":"2023-09-07T02:45:18Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"CoffeeDregs"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Reading this reminded me of an article in San Francisco Magazine from a couple of years ago:<p><a href=\"http://www.modernluxury.com/san-francisco/story/arise-tenderloin\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://www.modernluxury.com/san-francisco/story/arise-tender...</a><p>Quoting:<p>[...]<p>But the event that truly created the Tenderloin of today took place in 1980, when developers proposed three luxury high-rise hotels in the neighborhood. Residents and nonprofit advocacy groups organized to keep the big hotels out. The high rises were built, but in the aftermath of the fight, the city passed two crucial laws. First, the city made it illegal for owners to convert SROs into tourist hotels unless they also replaced the low-income units or paid into a fund for affordable housing. Second, it passed a zoning law lowering the height maximum in the Tenderloin from 30 stories to between 8 and 13.<p>These laws made it virtually impossible for developers to \u201cManhattanize\u201d the Tenderloin. Meanwhile, the nonprofits, which had lobbied for the laws and led the fight to keep out the big hotels, themselves began to buy or <em>lease</em> buildings. These nonprofits, which include the Tenderloin Neighborhood Development Corporation (TNDC), the Tenderloin Housing Clinic (THC), and Mercy Housing, now own or <em>lease</em> more than 5,000 housing units in the Tenderloin. The city plays a critical role too: It has loaned the nonprofits money to acquire or rehab about 50 properties with 4,000 affordable units, and it holds or <em>finances</em> master <em>leases</em> on dozens of other such buildings. \u201cThe Tenderloin is the only place where the nonprofits own the housing,\u201d says Field. \u201cThis protects the poor people in the neighborhood from changes in demographics.\u201d It also gives the nonprofits a vested interest in keeping their clients from being dispersed.<p>[...]<p>Combine that with rent control, resistance to new, higher density development and an influx of high paying tech jobs, and San Francisco's current situation makes a lot of sense...*<p>* To be fair, policies from the '80s that closed asylums and dumped mentally ill people onto the street contributed, but SF certainly seems to have worked to maximize the effect."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"In the Tenderloin"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://stories.californiasunday.com/2015-05-03/in-the-tenderloin/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_CoffeeDregs","story_9515872"],"author":"CoffeeDregs","children":[9517019],"comment_text":"Reading this reminded me of an article in San Francisco Magazine from a couple of years ago:<p><a href=\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.modernluxury.com&#x2F;san-francisco&#x2F;story&#x2F;arise-tenderloin\" rel=\"nofollow\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.modernluxury.com&#x2F;san-francisco&#x2F;story&#x2F;arise-tender...</a><p>Quoting:<p>[...]<p>But the event that truly created the Tenderloin of today took place in 1980, when developers proposed three luxury high-rise hotels in the neighborhood. Residents and nonprofit advocacy groups organized to keep the big hotels out. The high rises were built, but in the aftermath of the fight, the city passed two crucial laws. First, the city made it illegal for owners to convert SROs into tourist hotels unless they also replaced the low-income units or paid into a fund for affordable housing. Second, it passed a zoning law lowering the height maximum in the Tenderloin from 30 stories to between 8 and 13.<p>These laws made it virtually impossible for developers to \u201cManhattanize\u201d the Tenderloin. Meanwhile, the nonprofits, which had lobbied for the laws and led the fight to keep out the big hotels, themselves began to buy or lease buildings. These nonprofits, which include the Tenderloin Neighborhood Development Corporation (TNDC), the Tenderloin Housing Clinic (THC), and Mercy Housing, now own or lease more than 5,000 housing units in the Tenderloin. The city plays a critical role too: It has loaned the nonprofits money to acquire or rehab about 50 properties with 4,000 affordable units, and it holds or finances master leases on dozens of other such buildings. \u201cThe Tenderloin is the only place where the nonprofits own the housing,\u201d says Field. \u201cThis protects the poor people in the neighborhood from changes in demographics.\u201d It also gives the nonprofits a vested interest in keeping their clients from being dispersed.<p>[...]<p>Combine that with rent control, resistance to new, higher density development and an influx of high paying tech jobs, and San Francisco&#x27;s current situation makes a lot of sense...*<p>* To be fair, policies from the &#x27;80s that closed asylums and dumped mentally ill people onto the street contributed, but SF certainly seems to have worked to maximize the effect.","created_at":"2015-05-09T17:27:52Z","created_at_i":1431192472,"objectID":"9516998","parent_id":9515872,"story_id":9515872,"story_title":"In the Tenderloin","story_url":"https://stories.californiasunday.com/2015-05-03/in-the-tenderloin/","updated_at":"2024-09-19T21:46:29Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"techwatching"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"In regards to (1), they can <em>lease</em>/<em>finance</em> them. As long as the $60 includes enough gross margin, and as long as they have enough borrowing capacity, they don't need $2000 * X laptops to start up.<p>Seems like a good relationship with their financing partner would be key."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Technichi: Macbook Subscription Service"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://www.technichi.com/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_techwatching","story_7509409"],"author":"techwatching","comment_text":"In regards to (1), they can lease&#x2F;finance them. As long as the $60 includes enough gross margin, and as long as they have enough borrowing capacity, they don&#x27;t need $2000 * X laptops to start up.<p>Seems like a good relationship with their financing partner would be key.","created_at":"2014-04-01T18:30:33Z","created_at_i":1396377033,"objectID":"7510578","parent_id":7510291,"story_id":7509409,"story_title":"Technichi: Macbook Subscription Service","story_url":"http://www.technichi.com/","updated_at":"2024-09-19T20:41:34Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"dwild"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"&quot;zrail&quot; said in a comment that Apple already give that sort of offer to businesses:\n<a href=\"http://store.apple.com/us_smb_78313/browse/finance/lease\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://store.apple.com/us_smb_78313/browse/<em>finance</em>/<em>lease</em></a>"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Technichi: Macbook Subscription Service"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://www.technichi.com/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_dwild","story_7509409"],"author":"dwild","comment_text":"&quot;zrail&quot; said in a comment that Apple already give that sort of offer to businesses:\n<a href=\"http://store.apple.com/us_smb_78313/browse/finance/lease\" rel=\"nofollow\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;store.apple.com&#x2F;us_smb_78313&#x2F;browse&#x2F;finance&#x2F;lease</a>","created_at":"2014-04-01T18:14:44Z","created_at_i":1396376084,"objectID":"7510450","parent_id":7509934,"story_id":7509409,"story_title":"Technichi: Macbook Subscription Service","story_url":"http://www.technichi.com/","updated_at":"2024-09-19T20:41:34Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"zrail"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Apple itself has a Macbook &quot;subscription service&quot;, actually. It's called a &quot;Fair Market Value <em>lease</em>&quot; and works basically like a car <em>lease</em>.<p><a href=\"http://store.apple.com/us_smb_78313/browse/finance/lease\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://store.apple.com/us_smb_78313/browse/<em>finance</em>/<em>lease</em></a>"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Technichi: Macbook Subscription Service"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://www.technichi.com/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_zrail","story_7509409"],"author":"zrail","children":[7510330,7510410,7510509,7510792,7513004],"comment_text":"Apple itself has a Macbook &quot;subscription service&quot;, actually. It&#x27;s called a &quot;Fair Market Value lease&quot; and works basically like a car lease.<p><a href=\"http://store.apple.com/us_smb_78313/browse/finance/lease\" rel=\"nofollow\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;store.apple.com&#x2F;us_smb_78313&#x2F;browse&#x2F;finance&#x2F;lease</a>","created_at":"2014-04-01T17:24:11Z","created_at_i":1396373051,"objectID":"7509978","parent_id":7509409,"story_id":7509409,"story_title":"Technichi: Macbook Subscription Service","story_url":"http://www.technichi.com/","updated_at":"2024-09-19T20:41:28Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"davidu"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Some business models only work at scale.  Scale can be users, data, traffic, partnerships, distribution or a variety of things.<p>This is a bet that Foursquare will find a business model as they become the location service everything builds on, or something else, that only works at scale.<p>In the scheme of private equity financings, this is a rather small deal and makes perfect sense for Foursquare.<p>And in fact, buying equipment is one of the worst things to do with cash.  If you have the cash, then you should leverage that to <em>finance</em> or <em>lease</em> the equipment. :-)"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Foursquare raises $41 million Series D round"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://techcrunch.com/2013/04/11/foursquares-new-series-d-round-of-41m-helps-it-delay-tricky-questions-about-its-valuation/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_davidu","story_5531611"],"author":"davidu","children":[5532772],"comment_text":"Some business models only work at scale.  Scale can be users, data, traffic, partnerships, distribution or a variety of things.<p>This is a bet that Foursquare will find a business model as they become the location service everything builds on, or something else, that only works at scale.<p>In the scheme of private equity financings, this is a rather small deal and makes perfect sense for Foursquare.<p>And in fact, buying equipment is one of the worst things to do with cash.  If you have the cash, then you should leverage that to finance or lease the equipment. :-)","created_at":"2013-04-11T15:43:25Z","created_at_i":1365695005,"objectID":"5532674","parent_id":5532619,"points":null,"story_id":5531611,"story_title":"Foursquare raises $41 million Series D round","story_url":"http://techcrunch.com/2013/04/11/foursquares-new-series-d-round-of-41m-helps-it-delay-tricky-questions-about-its-valuation/","updated_at":"2023-09-06T21:16:12Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"codex"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"You're kidding, right?  They're making the best of a bad situation.  They'd love to have a normal leasing program like all other car companies, but they can't--they cannot <em>finance</em> <em>leases</em> themselves and no bank wants to risk losing money  reselling the car at the end of the lease.  The best they can do is offer to buy the car back at 40% of the purchase price in three years--and you have to take out a five year loan, put $10K down, and get that $10K back one year later, with none of the lower monthly payments of a normal lease.  This is strictly worse than the financing options from every other manufacturer."},"story_title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["finance"],"value":"Tesla Unveils New <em>Finance</em> Product"},"story_url":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["finance"],"value":"http://www.teslamotors.com/about/press/releases/tesla-unveils-revolutionary-new-<em>finance</em>-product"}},"_tags":["comment","author_codex","story_5482010"],"author":"codex","comment_text":"You're kidding, right?  They're making the best of a bad situation.  They'd love to have a normal leasing program like all other car companies, but they can't--they cannot finance leases themselves and no bank wants to risk losing money  reselling the car at the end of the lease.  The best they can do is offer to buy the car back at 40% of the purchase price in three years--and you have to take out a five year loan, put $10K down, and get that $10K back one year later, with none of the lower monthly payments of a normal lease.  This is strictly worse than the financing options from every other manufacturer.","created_at":"2013-04-03T03:16:16Z","created_at_i":1364958976,"objectID":"5484003","parent_id":5482345,"points":null,"story_id":5482010,"story_title":"Tesla Unveils New Finance Product","story_url":"http://www.teslamotors.com/about/press/releases/tesla-unveils-revolutionary-new-finance-product","updated_at":"2023-09-06T21:12:14Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"beachstartup"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"because tesla doesn't lend money, they make and sell the cars, they do not own them once they are sold<p>usually there is a separate entity that <em>finances</em> or <em>leases</em> the cars sold at the dealership, such as mbfs for mercedes benz financial services, gmac for general motors (defunct) etc<p>for tesla to offer the rebate directly they would have to setup a revenue split from the <em>finance</em> back end, or take a hit on the sales revenue, which is either difficult to get out of a bank, and not in the interest of tesla, respectively."},"story_title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["finance"],"value":"Tesla Unveils New <em>Finance</em> Product"},"story_url":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["finance"],"value":"http://www.teslamotors.com/about/press/releases/tesla-unveils-revolutionary-new-<em>finance</em>-product"}},"_tags":["comment","author_beachstartup","story_5482010"],"author":"beachstartup","comment_text":"because tesla doesn't lend money, they make and sell the cars, they do not own them once they are sold<p>usually there is a separate entity that finances or leases the cars sold at the dealership, such as mbfs for mercedes benz financial services, gmac for general motors (defunct) etc<p>for tesla to offer the rebate directly they would have to setup a revenue split from the finance back end, or take a hit on the sales revenue, which is either difficult to get out of a bank, and not in the interest of tesla, respectively.","created_at":"2013-04-02T23:52:59Z","created_at_i":1364946779,"objectID":"5483051","parent_id":5482453,"points":null,"story_id":5482010,"story_title":"Tesla Unveils New Finance Product","story_url":"http://www.teslamotors.com/about/press/releases/tesla-unveils-revolutionary-new-finance-product","updated_at":"2023-09-06T21:12:08Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"_aavaa_"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"&gt; Alphabet reported property-and-equipment purchases of about $51.4B, while analysts expected approximately $55.1B. Microsoft reported capex and <em>finance</em> <em>leases</em> of about $45.2B, while analysts were expecting around $50.7B. These were significant misses.<p>&gt;<p>&gt; [\u2026]<p>&gt;<p>&gt; Caterpillar inventories increased to $22.2 billion at the end of September [\u2026] The 22% increase in inventory signals future demand weakness or overproduction, and the reason is lower AI capex, as reported by Alphabet and Microsoft.<p>Maybe I\u2019m not getting it. But it\u2019s alphabet and Microsoft spending less than <i>someone external</i> said they should spend, not what they themselves said they would spend. How is that a miss?"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"AI Bubble Burst"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4960031-ai-bubble-burst-confirmed-by-alphabet-caterpillar-servicenow-amazon"}},"_tags":["comment","author__aavaa_","story_50303833"],"author":"_aavaa_","children":[50304885],"comment_text":"&gt; Alphabet reported property-and-equipment purchases of about $51.4B, while analysts expected approximately $55.1B. Microsoft reported capex and finance leases of about $45.2B, while analysts were expecting around $50.7B. These were significant misses.<p>&gt;<p>&gt; [\u2026]<p>&gt;<p>&gt; Caterpillar inventories increased to $22.2 billion at the end of September [\u2026] The 22% increase in inventory signals future demand weakness or overproduction, and the reason is lower AI capex, as reported by Alphabet and Microsoft.<p>Maybe I\u2019m not getting it. But it\u2019s alphabet and Microsoft spending less than <i>someone external</i> said they should spend, not what they themselves said they would spend. How is that a miss?","created_at":"2026-10-30T14:43:41Z","created_at_i":1793371421,"objectID":"50304674","parent_id":50303833,"story_id":50303833,"story_title":"AI Bubble Burst","story_url":"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4960031-ai-bubble-burst-confirmed-by-alphabet-caterpillar-servicenow-amazon","updated_at":"2026-10-30T15:02:05Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"buildsjets"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"This is the perfect route planning software if you are the only aircraft in the sky, and fuel is your only cost. The commercial solutions exist because they consider not only the winds and fuel costs in their optimization, but also known previously cleared ATC routings, altitude restrictions whether they are charted or only documented by letter-of-agreement, expected delays due to historical congestion, pilot and cabin crew pay, crew duty time limits (your more fuel efficient route may take longer and require an entire second crew), contracted <em>lease</em>, <em>finance</em>, insurance, and maintenance rates as these are also frequently paid by the the hour, and airport arrival curfew times. And probably a few more things too.<p>Also, you say nothing about incorporating ETOPS routing requirements into your application, and looking at the opentop source neither does it, therefore I assume this must mean this is the first time you have seen that acronym and therefore do not understand its criticality in determining aircraft routing. Might want to have your AI agent read this Wikipedia article, because your application generates many routes that are not legal to fly.<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ETOPS\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ETOPS</a><p>Should I even ask if this application routes around areas that have been declared active war zones by NOTAM?"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Saving Jet Fuel"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://tech.marksblogg.com/scikit-decide-openap-optimal-flight-planning.html"}},"_tags":["comment","author_buildsjets","story_49720164"],"author":"buildsjets","children":[49722287,49722558,49723438,49725136],"comment_text":"This is the perfect route planning software if you are the only aircraft in the sky, and fuel is your only cost. The commercial solutions exist because they consider not only the winds and fuel costs in their optimization, but also known previously cleared ATC routings, altitude restrictions whether they are charted or only documented by letter-of-agreement, expected delays due to historical congestion, pilot and cabin crew pay, crew duty time limits (your more fuel efficient route may take longer and require an entire second crew), contracted lease, finance, insurance, and maintenance rates as these are also frequently paid by the the hour, and airport arrival curfew times. And probably a few more things too.<p>Also, you say nothing about incorporating ETOPS routing requirements into your application, and looking at the opentop source neither does it, therefore I assume this must mean this is the first time you have seen that acronym and therefore do not understand its criticality in determining aircraft routing. Might want to have your AI agent read this Wikipedia article, because your application generates many routes that are not legal to fly.<p><a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;ETOPS\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;ETOPS</a><p>Should I even ask if this application routes around areas that have been declared active war zones by NOTAM?","created_at":"2026-09-16T04:22:51Z","created_at_i":1789532571,"objectID":"49722059","parent_id":49720164,"story_id":49720164,"story_title":"Saving Jet Fuel","story_url":"https://tech.marksblogg.com/scikit-decide-openap-optimal-flight-planning.html","updated_at":"2026-10-14T21:56:44Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"kotaKat"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"The other worse part is the fact that smartphone prices are skyrocketing and carriers are starting to signal they're no longer willing to bear the burden of 0% financing the phone to you based on the risks involved.<p>T-Mobile stepping out declaring &quot;nothing&quot; with their EIP Flex 36 that has a 0-24% creditworthiness-based interest and Klarna stepping in to do Apple leasing is a bad sign ahead.<p>So now it's not only thinking about &quot;buying a new phone&quot;... it's &quot;how are you paying for it now&quot;... will that be cash, a credit card, carrier <em>finance</em>, carrier <em>lease</em>, or your next of kin?"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"There's No Reason to Buy a New Phone Anymore"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/2026/08/secondhand-tech-boom/688309/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_kotaKat","story_49356878"],"author":"kotaKat","comment_text":"The other worse part is the fact that smartphone prices are skyrocketing and carriers are starting to signal they&#x27;re no longer willing to bear the burden of 0% financing the phone to you based on the risks involved.<p>T-Mobile stepping out declaring &quot;nothing&quot; with their EIP Flex 36 that has a 0-24% creditworthiness-based interest and Klarna stepping in to do Apple leasing is a bad sign ahead.<p>So now it&#x27;s not only thinking about &quot;buying a new phone&quot;... it&#x27;s &quot;how are you paying for it now&quot;... will that be cash, a credit card, carrier finance, carrier lease, or your next of kin?","created_at":"2026-08-19T12:00:54Z","created_at_i":1787140854,"objectID":"49360360","parent_id":49356878,"story_id":49356878,"story_title":"There's No Reason to Buy a New Phone Anymore","story_url":"https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/2026/08/secondhand-tech-boom/688309/","updated_at":"2026-08-19T13:35:39Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"vasco"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"This is not true at all, <em>leases</em> appear on balance sheets. It's not the 80s anymore<p>See Apple's FY2025 10-K, the <em>leases</em> are in page 42 under &quot;Lease-Related Assets and Liabilities&quot;, which shows:<p>Operating <em>leases</em><p>- Other current liabilities: $1,579 million<p>- Other non-current liabilities: $10,911 million<p><em>Finance</em> <em>leases</em><p>- Other current liabilities: $538 million<p>- Other non-current liabilities: $692 million<p>Total lease liabilities: $13,720 million<p><a href=\"https://s2.q4cdn.com/470004039/files/doc_financials/2025/ar/_10-K-2025-As-Filed.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://s2.q4cdn.com/470004039/files/doc_financials/2025/ar/...</a>"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Five US tech giants' hidden debts soar to $1.65T on opaque AI funding"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://asia.nikkei.com/business/technology/five-us-tech-giants-hidden-debts-soar-to-1.65tn-on-opaque-ai-funding"}},"_tags":["comment","author_vasco","story_48987863"],"author":"vasco","children":[48988922,48996273],"comment_text":"This is not true at all, leases appear on balance sheets. It&#x27;s not the 80s anymore<p>See Apple&#x27;s FY2025 10-K, the leases are in page 42 under &quot;Lease-Related Assets and Liabilities&quot;, which shows:<p>Operating leases<p>- Other current liabilities: $1,579 million<p>- Other non-current liabilities: $10,911 million<p>Finance leases<p>- Other current liabilities: $538 million<p>- Other non-current liabilities: $692 million<p>Total lease liabilities: $13,720 million<p><a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;s2.q4cdn.com&#x2F;470004039&#x2F;files&#x2F;doc_financials&#x2F;2025&#x2F;ar&#x2F;_10-K-2025-As-Filed.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;s2.q4cdn.com&#x2F;470004039&#x2F;files&#x2F;doc_financials&#x2F;2025&#x2F;ar&#x2F;...</a>","created_at":"2026-07-21T06:22:31Z","created_at_i":1784614951,"objectID":"48988744","parent_id":48988618,"story_id":48987863,"story_title":"Five US tech giants' hidden debts soar to $1.65T on opaque AI funding","story_url":"https://asia.nikkei.com/business/technology/five-us-tech-giants-hidden-debts-soar-to-1.65tn-on-opaque-ai-funding","updated_at":"2026-07-22T01:07:58Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Eldodi"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Crazy this company will IPO for &gt;1B with such bad financials! That said, Starlink seems to be a real cash machine, not as good as ads but enough to support AI bets.<p>2025:<p>- Revenue: $18.7B, up from $14.0B in 2024<p>- Operating loss: -$2.6B<p>- Net loss: -$4.9B<p>- Adjusted EBITDA: $6.6B<p>- Operating cash flow: $6.8B<p>- Capex: $20.7B<p>Segment breakdown:<p>- Starlink / Connectivity: $11.4B revenue, $4.4B operating income, $7.2B adj. EBITDA<p>- Space / launch: $4.1B revenue, -$657M operating loss<p>- AI / xAI / X: $3.2B revenue, -$6.4B operating loss<p>Starlink metrics:<p>- Subscribers: 8.9M at end-2025, 10.3M by Mar 31 2026<p>- ARPU: $99/month in 2023, $81 in 2025, $66 in Q1 2026<p>Balance sheet as of Mar 31 2026:<p>- Cash: $15.9B<p>- Marketable securities: $7.8B<p>- Total assets: $102.1B<p>- Total liabilities: $60.5B<p>- Debt / <em>finance</em> <em>leases</em>: about $30.3B"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"SpaceX S-1"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1181412/000162828026036936/spaceexplorationtechnologi.htm"}},"_tags":["comment","author_Eldodi","story_48213933"],"author":"Eldodi","children":[48214212,48214420,48214498,48214538,48214543,48214669,48215141,48215218,48215798,48216263,48216868],"comment_text":"Crazy this company will IPO for &gt;1B with such bad financials! That said, Starlink seems to be a real cash machine, not as good as ads but enough to support AI bets.<p>2025:<p>- Revenue: $18.7B, up from $14.0B in 2024<p>- Operating loss: -$2.6B<p>- Net loss: -$4.9B<p>- Adjusted EBITDA: $6.6B<p>- Operating cash flow: $6.8B<p>- Capex: $20.7B<p>Segment breakdown:<p>- Starlink &#x2F; Connectivity: $11.4B revenue, $4.4B operating income, $7.2B adj. EBITDA<p>- Space &#x2F; launch: $4.1B revenue, -$657M operating loss<p>- AI &#x2F; xAI &#x2F; X: $3.2B revenue, -$6.4B operating loss<p>Starlink metrics:<p>- Subscribers: 8.9M at end-2025, 10.3M by Mar 31 2026<p>- ARPU: $99&#x2F;month in 2023, $81 in 2025, $66 in Q1 2026<p>Balance sheet as of Mar 31 2026:<p>- Cash: $15.9B<p>- Marketable securities: $7.8B<p>- Total assets: $102.1B<p>- Total liabilities: $60.5B<p>- Debt &#x2F; finance leases: about $30.3B","created_at":"2026-05-20T21:00:00Z","created_at_i":1779310800,"objectID":"48214062","parent_id":48213933,"story_id":48213933,"story_title":"SpaceX S-1","story_url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1181412/000162828026036936/spaceexplorationtechnologi.htm","updated_at":"2026-05-23T16:02:29Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"jpfromlondon"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"It was common (at least here in europe) to simply buy your car, with the proliferation of <em>finance</em> and <em>lease</em> it is now the norm to essentially rent your own car for two to four years before inevitably having to trade it in for another, perpetually on the strap.<p>This has driven the actual price of vehicles both used and new to astronomical levels under the assumption that you will have it on-plan rather than own it outright.<p>Major things aside, it's the every day minor erosions in qol that depress me...for example cereal boxes used to be stiffer, chocolate had more cocoa solids, products were on average better quality, to get that same quality requires you to know and spend a lot more, we aren't imagining it, there was a sweet spot somewhere between 2003 and 2016."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"I built \"Middle Class Museum\", a tour of things that used to be affordable"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.ideagames.fun/middle-class-museum"}},"_tags":["comment","author_jpfromlondon","story_47932685"],"author":"jpfromlondon","comment_text":"It was common (at least here in europe) to simply buy your car, with the proliferation of finance and lease it is now the norm to essentially rent your own car for two to four years before inevitably having to trade it in for another, perpetually on the strap.<p>This has driven the actual price of vehicles both used and new to astronomical levels under the assumption that you will have it on-plan rather than own it outright.<p>Major things aside, it&#x27;s the every day minor erosions in qol that depress me...for example cereal boxes used to be stiffer, chocolate had more cocoa solids, products were on average better quality, to get that same quality requires you to know and spend a lot more, we aren&#x27;t imagining it, there was a sweet spot somewhere between 2003 and 2016.","created_at":"2026-04-29T11:01:54Z","created_at_i":1777460514,"objectID":"47946627","parent_id":47935435,"story_id":47932685,"story_title":"I built \"Middle Class Museum\", a tour of things that used to be affordable","story_url":"https://www.ideagames.fun/middle-class-museum","updated_at":"2026-04-29T11:03:02Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"zerosizedweasle"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Meta owns 20%, and funds managed by Blue Owl own the other 80%. Last month, a holding company called Beignet Investor, which owns the Blue Owl portion, sold a then-record $27.3 billion of bonds to investors, mostly to Pimco.\nMeta said it won\u2019t be consolidating the joint venture, meaning the venture\u2019s assets and liabilities will remain off Meta\u2019s balance sheet. Instead Meta will rent the data center for as long as 20 years, beginning in 2029. But it will start with a four-year <em>lease</em> term, with options to renew every four years.<p>This <em>lease</em> structure minimizes the <em>lease</em> liabilities and related assets Meta will recognize, and enables Meta to use \u201coperating <em>lease</em>,\u201d rather than \u201c<em>finance</em> <em>lease</em>,\u201d treatment. If Meta used the latter, it would look more like Meta owns the asset and is financing it with debt.<p>The favorable accounting outcome hinges on some convenient assumptions. Some appear implausible, while others are in tension with one another, making the off-balance-sheet treatment look questionable.<p><a href=\"https://www.wsj.com/tech/meta-ai-data-center-finances-d3a6b464\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.wsj.com/tech/meta-ai-data-center-finances-d3a6b4...</a>"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Nvidia's 'I'm Not Enron' memo has people asking a lot of questions"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.theverge.com/business/828047/nvidia-enron-conspiracy-accounting"}},"_tags":["comment","author_zerosizedweasle","story_46044126"],"author":"zerosizedweasle","comment_text":"Meta owns 20%, and funds managed by Blue Owl own the other 80%. Last month, a holding company called Beignet Investor, which owns the Blue Owl portion, sold a then-record $27.3 billion of bonds to investors, mostly to Pimco.\nMeta said it won\u2019t be consolidating the joint venture, meaning the venture\u2019s assets and liabilities will remain off Meta\u2019s balance sheet. Instead Meta will rent the data center for as long as 20 years, beginning in 2029. But it will start with a four-year lease term, with options to renew every four years.<p>This lease structure minimizes the lease liabilities and related assets Meta will recognize, and enables Meta to use \u201coperating lease,\u201d rather than \u201cfinance lease,\u201d treatment. If Meta used the latter, it would look more like Meta owns the asset and is financing it with debt.<p>The favorable accounting outcome hinges on some convenient assumptions. Some appear implausible, while others are in tension with one another, making the off-balance-sheet treatment look questionable.<p><a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.wsj.com&#x2F;tech&#x2F;meta-ai-data-center-finances-d3a6b464\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.wsj.com&#x2F;tech&#x2F;meta-ai-data-center-finances-d3a6b4...</a>","created_at":"2025-11-25T09:48:31Z","created_at_i":1764064111,"objectID":"46044176","parent_id":46044126,"story_id":46044126,"story_title":"Nvidia's 'I'm Not Enron' memo has people asking a lot of questions","story_url":"https://www.theverge.com/business/828047/nvidia-enron-conspiracy-accounting","updated_at":"2026-03-05T23:07:13Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"queenkjuul"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Frighteningly common attitude amongst Americans, including my parents. Never made sense to me. I get that American cities are basically designed to require a car, but that doesn't mean you need to <em>finance</em> or <em>lease</em> something brand new all the time."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"The Friendship Recession: The lost art of connecting"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.happiness.hks.harvard.edu/february-2025-issue/the-friendship-recession-the-lost-art-of-connecting"}},"_tags":["comment","author_queenkjuul","story_43802727"],"author":"queenkjuul","comment_text":"Frighteningly common attitude amongst Americans, including my parents. Never made sense to me. I get that American cities are basically designed to require a car, but that doesn&#x27;t mean you need to finance or lease something brand new all the time.","created_at":"2025-04-27T19:05:06Z","created_at_i":1745780706,"objectID":"43814312","parent_id":43808016,"story_id":43802727,"story_title":"The Friendship Recession: The lost art of connecting","story_url":"https://www.happiness.hks.harvard.edu/february-2025-issue/the-friendship-recession-the-lost-art-of-connecting","updated_at":"2025-04-29T02:42:07Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"hackernews1134"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"&gt; Amazon\u2019s operating income in 2024 improved 86% YoY, from $36.9B (an operating margin of 6.4%) to $68.6B (an operating margin of 10.8%). Free Cash Flow, adjusted for equipment <em>finance</em> <em>leases</em> improved from $35.5B in 2023 to $36.2B.<p>Can someone help me understand these numbers. Their operating income improved (increased?) by 86% but margin only ~4%?<p>Does that mean most of the improved (increased?) operating income was put towards capital outflow?"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Amazon CEO Andy Jassy's 2024 Letter to Shareholders"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-2024-letter-to-shareholders"}},"_tags":["comment","author_hackernews1134","story_43643408"],"author":"hackernews1134","comment_text":"&gt; Amazon\u2019s operating income in 2024 improved 86% YoY, from $36.9B (an operating margin of 6.4%) to $68.6B (an operating margin of 10.8%). Free Cash Flow, adjusted for equipment finance leases improved from $35.5B in 2023 to $36.2B.<p>Can someone help me understand these numbers. Their operating income improved (increased?) by 86% but margin only ~4%?<p>Does that mean most of the improved (increased?) operating income was put towards capital outflow?","created_at":"2025-04-11T09:05:00Z","created_at_i":1744362300,"objectID":"43651864","parent_id":43643408,"story_id":43643408,"story_title":"Amazon CEO Andy Jassy's 2024 Letter to Shareholders","story_url":"https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/amazon-ceo-andy-jassy-2024-letter-to-shareholders","updated_at":"2025-04-11T09:08:37Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"cosmic_cheese"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"I would say the used market starts to be more of a factor at $15k and below. Right now the value proposition of cars in the $15-25k range is truly awful, with it often making more sense to <em>finance</em> or <em>lease</em> a new car instead. When I was shopping last year I was seeing used Honda Fits that were the better part of a decade old north of the $15k mark, which is absurd considering they didn\u2019t cost much more than that new. It\u2019s even worse for in-demand models like RAV4\u2019s."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"A number of electric vehicle, battery factories are being canceled"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2025/04/03/ev-factories-canceled/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_cosmic_cheese","story_43583249"],"author":"cosmic_cheese","children":[43584088],"comment_text":"I would say the used market starts to be more of a factor at $15k and below. Right now the value proposition of cars in the $15-25k range is truly awful, with it often making more sense to finance or lease a new car instead. When I was shopping last year I was seeing used Honda Fits that were the better part of a decade old north of the $15k mark, which is absurd considering they didn\u2019t cost much more than that new. It\u2019s even worse for in-demand models like RAV4\u2019s.","created_at":"2025-04-04T15:31:23Z","created_at_i":1743780683,"objectID":"43583887","parent_id":43583831,"story_id":43583249,"story_title":"A number of electric vehicle, battery factories are being canceled","story_url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2025/04/03/ev-factories-canceled/","updated_at":"2025-04-04T16:52:38Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"declaredapple"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"&gt; What we're trying to find is a business that, for one reason or another -- it can be because it's the low-cost producer in some area, it can be because it has a natural franchise because of surface capabilities, it could be because of its position in the consumers' mind, it can be because of a technological advantage, or any kind of reason at all, that it has this moat around it.<p>He didn't seem to have specific definition at all really.<p>I think most people attribute it to a &quot;secret sauce technology&quot; in the case of OpenAI, I'm not sure if &quot;<em>finances</em> to <em>lease</em> a huge cluster of GPUs&quot; makes sense here because the main competitors (Google, AWS, Apple, etc) also have access to insane compute as well yet have struggled to get close to GPT4's performance in practice.<p>That said I do agree that it's a moat for the startups like stability/mistral, etc. They also have access to $/compute, albiet a lot less. And you can see this in their research, as they've been focused on methods to lower the training/inference costs."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Sora: Creating video from text"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://openai.com/sora"}},"_tags":["comment","author_declaredapple","story_39386156"],"author":"declaredapple","comment_text":"&gt; What we&#x27;re trying to find is a business that, for one reason or another -- it can be because it&#x27;s the low-cost producer in some area, it can be because it has a natural franchise because of surface capabilities, it could be because of its position in the consumers&#x27; mind, it can be because of a technological advantage, or any kind of reason at all, that it has this moat around it.<p>He didn&#x27;t seem to have specific definition at all really.<p>I think most people attribute it to a &quot;secret sauce technology&quot; in the case of OpenAI, I&#x27;m not sure if &quot;finances to lease a huge cluster of GPUs&quot; makes sense here because the main competitors (Google, AWS, Apple, etc) also have access to insane compute as well yet have struggled to get close to GPT4&#x27;s performance in practice.<p>That said I do agree that it&#x27;s a moat for the startups like stability&#x2F;mistral, etc. They also have access to $&#x2F;compute, albiet a lot less. And you can see this in their research, as they&#x27;ve been focused on methods to lower the training&#x2F;inference costs.","created_at":"2024-02-15T19:58:14Z","created_at_i":1708027094,"objectID":"39387783","parent_id":39387663,"story_id":39386156,"story_title":"Sora: Creating video from text","story_url":"https://openai.com/sora","updated_at":"2024-09-20T16:30:07Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"b112"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"That's a good point.  Likely, rates have mostly peaked.  There will potentially be downward pressure soon.<p>On top of that, many cars are hard to get.  Low production, backlogged demand.  This means fewer deals on <em>finance</em> and <em>lease</em> rates.<p>I remember 0% <em>lease</em> and <em>finance</em> rates, 15 years ago when the interest rate was this high, because car companies wanted to move product.  But now they have no need, not with them unable to supply demand.<p>So my point is, I decided to keep my current car longer.  Rates matter."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Volkswagen cuts jobs as demand for EVs plunges"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://fortune.com/2023/09/14/vw-cutting-jobs-german-ev-factory-because-demand-plunging-china-tesla/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_b112","story_37637715"],"author":"b112","children":[37638292],"comment_text":"That&#x27;s a good point.  Likely, rates have mostly peaked.  There will potentially be downward pressure soon.<p>On top of that, many cars are hard to get.  Low production, backlogged demand.  This means fewer deals on finance and lease rates.<p>I remember 0% lease and finance rates, 15 years ago when the interest rate was this high, because car companies wanted to move product.  But now they have no need, not with them unable to supply demand.<p>So my point is, I decided to keep my current car longer.  Rates matter.","created_at":"2023-09-24T23:21:28Z","created_at_i":1695597688,"objectID":"37638103","parent_id":37637965,"story_id":37637715,"story_title":"Volkswagen cuts jobs as demand for EVs plunges","story_url":"https://fortune.com/2023/09/14/vw-cutting-jobs-german-ev-factory-because-demand-plunging-china-tesla/","updated_at":"2025-12-14T20:35:08Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"wpsimon3"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"I always assumed they could be used by the dealer to &quot;mark&quot; a car that was delinquent on its <em>lease</em>/<em>finance</em> payments."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"All of the vehicle license plates available in America"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.beautifulpublicdata.com/all-of-the-license-plates-in-the-united-states/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_wpsimon3","story_37209888"],"author":"wpsimon3","comment_text":"I always assumed they could be used by the dealer to &quot;mark&quot; a car that was delinquent on its lease&#x2F;finance payments.","created_at":"2023-08-22T04:19:58Z","created_at_i":1692677998,"objectID":"37218643","parent_id":37215146,"story_id":37209888,"story_title":"All of the vehicle license plates available in America","story_url":"https://www.beautifulpublicdata.com/all-of-the-license-plates-in-the-united-states/","updated_at":"2024-09-20T14:56:14Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"codegeek"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"You will be surprised to know that lot of real wealthy people drive normal cars. People who buy flashy cars in America, majority of them <em>finance</em>/<em>lease</em> it and probably couldn't afford it to buy in cash which is what u need to be able to do if ur buying a car."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"New York City will charge drivers going downtown"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.cnn.com/2023/06/10/business/congestion-pricing-new-york-city-transportation/index.html"}},"_tags":["comment","author_codegeek","story_36270597"],"author":"codegeek","comment_text":"You will be surprised to know that lot of real wealthy people drive normal cars. People who buy flashy cars in America, majority of them finance&#x2F;lease it and probably couldn&#x27;t afford it to buy in cash which is what u need to be able to do if ur buying a car.","created_at":"2023-06-10T21:35:37Z","created_at_i":1686432937,"objectID":"36275389","parent_id":36274139,"story_id":36270597,"story_title":"New York City will charge drivers going downtown","story_url":"https://www.cnn.com/2023/06/10/business/congestion-pricing-new-york-city-transportation/index.html","updated_at":"2024-09-20T14:17:44Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"joelhaus"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"My father <em>leases</em>/<em>finances</em> equipment and occasionally gets the used machines back after the term expires. Was thinking that a website positioning the vending machines as a great learning tool for kids would be an interesting way to sell them. Depending on the popularity, I imagine you might even be able to build some web applications that would help the kids manage and find locations for the machines (a sort of marketplace for machine owners and location owners). There's the possibility of upgrading the machines and even adding NFC support to older ones too.<p>Am looking into how many machines I can get my hands on now..."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"My dad taught me cashflow with a soda machine"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"http://blog.thestartuptoolkit.com/2011/10/my-dad-taught-me-cashflow-with-a-soda-machine/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_joelhaus","story_3167630"],"author":"joelhaus","children":[3170533],"comment_text":"My father leases/finances equipment and occasionally gets the used machines back after the term expires. Was thinking that a website positioning the vending machines as a great learning tool for kids would be an interesting way to sell them. Depending on the popularity, I imagine you might even be able to build some web applications that would help the kids manage and find locations for the machines (a sort of marketplace for machine owners and location owners). There's the possibility of upgrading the machines and even adding NFC support to older ones too.<p>Am looking into how many machines I can get my hands on now...","created_at":"2011-10-28T18:11:06Z","created_at_i":1319825466,"objectID":"3169380","parent_id":3167877,"points":null,"story_id":3167630,"story_title":"My dad taught me cashflow with a soda machine","story_url":"http://blog.thestartuptoolkit.com/2011/10/my-dad-taught-me-cashflow-with-a-soda-machine/","updated_at":"2023-09-06T20:33:59Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"2Gkashmiri"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"<em>leases</em> are of two kinds technically. operating and <em>finance</em> lease.<p>one gives you ownership at the start, the second one merely &quot;rents&quot; you the thing"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Eu.org, free domain names since 1996"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://nic.eu.org/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_2Gkashmiri","story_31650320"],"author":"2Gkashmiri","comment_text":"leases are of two kinds technically. operating and finance lease.<p>one gives you ownership at the start, the second one merely &quot;rents&quot; you the thing","created_at":"2022-06-07T08:09:16Z","created_at_i":1654589356,"objectID":"31651196","parent_id":31650999,"story_id":31650320,"story_title":"Eu.org, free domain names since 1996","story_url":"https://nic.eu.org/","updated_at":"2024-09-20T11:18:45Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"dane-pgp"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Except the government has only done the bare minimum for leasehold reform, and has seemingly abandoned the more ambitious plans it had promised for this session of parliament[0].  As for the Leasehold Reform (Ground Rent) Act 2022, it's worth reading the small print:<p>&quot;Certain types of <em>leases</em> are also excepted by the LRGRA 2022 such as business <em>leases</em>, statutory lease extensions of houses and flats, community housing <em>leases</em> and home <em>finance</em> plan <em>leases</em>. The LRGRA 2022 will not apply to <em>leases</em> granted under contracts entered into prior to the commencement of the Act, unless in accordance with an option or right of first refusal.&quot;[1]<p>[0] <a href=\"https://nationalleaseholdcampaign.org/leasehold-reform-broken-promises/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://nationalleaseholdcampaign.org/leasehold-reform-broke...</a><p>[1] <a href=\"https://www.keystonelaw.com/keynotes/the-end-of-residential-ground-rents-comes-into-force-on-30-june-2022\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.keystonelaw.com/keynotes/the-end-of-residential-...</a>"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Who owns Parliament?"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://whoownsengland.org/2016/09/06/who-owns-parliament/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_dane-pgp","story_31574858"],"author":"dane-pgp","comment_text":"Except the government has only done the bare minimum for leasehold reform, and has seemingly abandoned the more ambitious plans it had promised for this session of parliament[0].  As for the Leasehold Reform (Ground Rent) Act 2022, it&#x27;s worth reading the small print:<p>&quot;Certain types of leases are also excepted by the LRGRA 2022 such as business leases, statutory lease extensions of houses and flats, community housing leases and home finance plan leases. The LRGRA 2022 will not apply to leases granted under contracts entered into prior to the commencement of the Act, unless in accordance with an option or right of first refusal.&quot;[1]<p>[0] <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;nationalleaseholdcampaign.org&#x2F;leasehold-reform-broken-promises&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;nationalleaseholdcampaign.org&#x2F;leasehold-reform-broke...</a><p>[1] <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.keystonelaw.com&#x2F;keynotes&#x2F;the-end-of-residential-ground-rents-comes-into-force-on-30-june-2022\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.keystonelaw.com&#x2F;keynotes&#x2F;the-end-of-residential-...</a>","created_at":"2022-06-02T17:54:48Z","created_at_i":1654192488,"objectID":"31598277","parent_id":31597906,"story_id":31574858,"story_title":"Who owns Parliament?","story_url":"https://whoownsengland.org/2016/09/06/who-owns-parliament/","updated_at":"2024-09-20T11:20:09Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"adolph"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Pretty sure this is also not good news for the leasing companies and the airplane manufacturers (aside from many of them making it up on increased defense spending).<p><i>The European country most exposed to this risk is certainly Ireland. According to the Irish Times newspaper,  the leasing company Aercap owns 149 aircraft operated in Russia and SMBC Aviation Capital has 34 jets leased to Russian operators.</i> [0]<p><i>AerCap is the world's largest aircraft leasing company after acquiring International <em>Lease</em> <em>Finance</em> Corporation in 2014. As of June 2020, AerCap had 1,035 owned, managed aircraft in its portfolio.</i> [1]<p>That's 14% of their business that got nuked.<p>0. <a href=\"https://airlinegeeks.com/2022/03/02/lessors-look-to-repatriate-aircraft-from-russia/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://airlinegeeks.com/2022/03/02/lessors-look-to-repatria...</a><p>1. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AerCap\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AerCap</a>"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Sabre ends distribution agreement with Aeroflot"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.cnbc.com/2022/03/03/airline-software-giant-sabre-ends-service-with-russias-aeroflot-crippling-carriers-ability-to-sell-seats.html"}},"_tags":["comment","author_adolph","story_30542120"],"author":"adolph","comment_text":"Pretty sure this is also not good news for the leasing companies and the airplane manufacturers (aside from many of them making it up on increased defense spending).<p><i>The European country most exposed to this risk is certainly Ireland. According to the Irish Times newspaper,  the leasing company Aercap owns 149 aircraft operated in Russia and SMBC Aviation Capital has 34 jets leased to Russian operators.</i> [0]<p><i>AerCap is the world&#x27;s largest aircraft leasing company after acquiring International Lease Finance Corporation in 2014. As of June 2020, AerCap had 1,035 owned, managed aircraft in its portfolio.</i> [1]<p>That&#x27;s 14% of their business that got nuked.<p>0. <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;airlinegeeks.com&#x2F;2022&#x2F;03&#x2F;02&#x2F;lessors-look-to-repatriate-aircraft-from-russia&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;airlinegeeks.com&#x2F;2022&#x2F;03&#x2F;02&#x2F;lessors-look-to-repatria...</a><p>1. <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;AerCap\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;AerCap</a>","created_at":"2022-03-03T20:41:49Z","created_at_i":1646340109,"objectID":"30546671","parent_id":30545743,"story_id":30542120,"story_title":"Sabre ends distribution agreement with Aeroflot","story_url":"https://www.cnbc.com/2022/03/03/airline-software-giant-sabre-ends-service-with-russias-aeroflot-crippling-carriers-ability-to-sell-seats.html","updated_at":"2024-09-20T10:36:58Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"danielmarkbruce"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"Their filings don't suggest that. If you have specific examples I'd certainly be interested to hear them or see a link. They were doing physical only commerce for years before anything digital and it wasn't profitable. Now that's understandable, but the claim that physical e-commerce was massively profitable seems very overstated.<p>When you say &quot;digital&quot; do you mean like Prime Video? Or kindle books? Or something else? When you say devices do you mean kindles? I'm struggling to believe that the kindle book line is run at a loss. I'd understand specific titles might run at a loss and I'd understand if the devices run at a loss but kindle books overall? Prime Video &quot;losses&quot; are sort of my point - they can almost be thought of as marketing expense.<p>As for the capital <em>leases</em> - those are accounted for at the corporate level and mostly run through the cashflow statement. The numbers they quote as operating income for  the non aws business don't include those capital lease costs. ie on page 17 of their most recent Q (<a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/ix?doc=/Archives/edgar/data/1018724/000101872421000028/amzn-20210930.htm\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.sec.gov/ix?doc=/Archives/edgar/data/1018724/0001...</a>) those reported operating earnings don't include the capital <em>leases</em>. If you look on page 3 of that filing (Principal repayment of <em>finance</em> <em>leases</em>) you'll see the (big) number there.<p>All that said - you still could be right. But if you are it's almost like they are going out of their way to deliberately make their numbers look bad on the non-AWS side. There is a big difference between not caring and actively making it look bad."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Amazon and the \u201cprofitless business model\u201d fallacy (2013)"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.eugenewei.com/blog/2013/10/25/amazon-and-the-profitless-business-model-narrative"}},"_tags":["comment","author_danielmarkbruce","story_29661261"],"author":"danielmarkbruce","children":[29679727],"comment_text":"Their filings don&#x27;t suggest that. If you have specific examples I&#x27;d certainly be interested to hear them or see a link. They were doing physical only commerce for years before anything digital and it wasn&#x27;t profitable. Now that&#x27;s understandable, but the claim that physical e-commerce was massively profitable seems very overstated.<p>When you say &quot;digital&quot; do you mean like Prime Video? Or kindle books? Or something else? When you say devices do you mean kindles? I&#x27;m struggling to believe that the kindle book line is run at a loss. I&#x27;d understand specific titles might run at a loss and I&#x27;d understand if the devices run at a loss but kindle books overall? Prime Video &quot;losses&quot; are sort of my point - they can almost be thought of as marketing expense.<p>As for the capital leases - those are accounted for at the corporate level and mostly run through the cashflow statement. The numbers they quote as operating income for  the non aws business don&#x27;t include those capital lease costs. ie on page 17 of their most recent Q (<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.sec.gov&#x2F;ix?doc=&#x2F;Archives&#x2F;edgar&#x2F;data&#x2F;1018724&#x2F;000101872421000028&#x2F;amzn-20210930.htm\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.sec.gov&#x2F;ix?doc=&#x2F;Archives&#x2F;edgar&#x2F;data&#x2F;1018724&#x2F;0001...</a>) those reported operating earnings don&#x27;t include the capital leases. If you look on page 3 of that filing (Principal repayment of finance leases) you&#x27;ll see the (big) number there.<p>All that said - you still could be right. But if you are it&#x27;s almost like they are going out of their way to deliberately make their numbers look bad on the non-AWS side. There is a big difference between not caring and actively making it look bad.","created_at":"2021-12-24T16:56:35Z","created_at_i":1640364995,"objectID":"29675178","parent_id":29670546,"story_id":29661261,"story_title":"Amazon and the \u201cprofitless business model\u201d fallacy (2013)","story_url":"https://www.eugenewei.com/blog/2013/10/25/amazon-and-the-profitless-business-model-narrative","updated_at":"2024-09-20T10:05:22Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"giobox"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["finance","leases"],"value":"It used to be quite common; at various times in its history you can make the argument Ford sold cars at a loss to help Ford Credit sell profitable car loans. Even today, Ford Credit (loans/<em>leases</em> etc) is responsible for about 50% of Ford's annual profits:<p>&gt; <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Motor_Credit_Company\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Motor_Credit_Company</a><p>There have been quite a few years Ford made more profit as a &quot;bank&quot; than they did from making and selling the cars. If you get a new Ford from a dealer and request <em>finance</em>/lease options, its pretty typical for Ford Credit to provide the backing."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Tesla remotely unlocks Model 3 car, uses smart summon to help repo agent"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://tiremeetsroad.com/2021/03/18/tesla-allegedly-remotely-unlocks-model-3-owners-car-uses-smart-summon-to-help-repo-agent/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_giobox","story_29623658"],"author":"giobox","comment_text":"It used to be quite common; at various times in its history you can make the argument Ford sold cars at a loss to help Ford Credit sell profitable car loans. Even today, Ford Credit (loans&#x2F;leases etc) is responsible for about 50% of Ford&#x27;s annual profits:<p>&gt; <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;Ford_Motor_Credit_Company\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;Ford_Motor_Credit_Company</a><p>There have been quite a few years Ford made more profit as a &quot;bank&quot; than they did from making and selling the cars. If you get a new Ford from a dealer and request finance&#x2F;lease options, its pretty typical for Ford Credit to provide the backing.","created_at":"2021-12-20T19:41:21Z","created_at_i":1640029281,"objectID":"29629163","parent_id":29626717,"story_id":29623658,"story_title":"Tesla remotely unlocks Model 3 car, uses smart summon to help repo agent","story_url":"https://tiremeetsroad.com/2021/03/18/tesla-allegedly-remotely-unlocks-model-3-owners-car-uses-smart-summon-to-help-repo-agent/","updated_at":"2024-09-20T10:00:59Z"}],"hitsPerPage":30,"nbHits":963,"nbPages":33,"page":0,"params":"query=finance+leases&tags=comment&hitsPerPage=30&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":34,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"roundTrip":14},"afterFetch":{"format":{"highlighting":2,"total":3},"merge":{"mergeLoop":{"prepareNextHit":7,"total":7},"total":7},"total":7},"fetch":{"query":7,"scanning":17,"total":25},"total":34},"query":"finance leases","serverTimeMS":38}
